Description: antinomie: hook the x86-64 architecture
 Everything the series changes in the x86 architecture's own code, kept
 in one patch so that the rest of the series is the same on every
 architecture: a port adds a patch like this one, its <asm/antinomie.h>
 and generated <asm/antinomie_syscalls.h>, and its per-architecture
 files. Without it the series builds, but records nothing and has no
 transport.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - arch/x86/entry/common.c calls the recorder at system call entry and
   exit, in do_syscall_64() and the 32-bit entry, the only places that
   see every call with its registers. A 32-bit call is recorded with
   its ABI and no table.
 - arch/x86/kernel/setup.c reserves the bootstrap page from memblock
   right after the e820 map is set up, before the kernel allocates
   memory, so nothing else takes it; and takes the antinomie_* boot
   options out of the command line before the kernel first prints it.
 - arch/x86/boot/compressed/kaslr.c and the x86 EFI stub
   (drivers/firmware/efi/libstub/x86-stub.c) keep the decompressor's
   and the EFI stub's random placement of the kernel off the bootstrap
   page. Either could otherwise put the kernel on it, and the transport
   would never come up.
 .
 Contract: unchanged (revision 17).
Author: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
